#1042 is fixed and committed as ff1ed5c9. After a /cd, /spawn now leaves out any instruction file that the new directory's [directories] deny or allow list refuses, and prints a warning. Build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the format check all pass.

The fix: in src/context.rs, the per-file check in the instruction-file loader used check_path, which only knows the startup fence. It now goes through cd_config_note::path_guard_refusal_lists, the same guard the file tools use: startup fence first, then the /cd lists. Both the startup load and /spawn use it. At startup no /cd has run, so the output there is byte-for-byte the same as before. .yoyo/memory.json goes through the same check, so it is fenced the same way.

Tests (src/context_fence_tests.rs, 4 new). They pass the /cd lists in directly and never touch the process-wide lists:
• A /cd deny over the directory keeps the canary in CLAUDE.md out of the loaded context, and the refusal is reported. A check confirms the fixture really contains the canary.
• A /cd allow list that doesn't cover the directory also keeps it out.
• Two near-miss cases: empty /cd lists, and a deny over a different directory. Both produce exactly the same output as the unfenced loader.

Positive control: I put the bare check_path back, ran the tests and restored the fix in one command. The two refusal tests and no_check_path_reader_skips_the_cd_deny_list_unregistered failed by name, and the near-miss tests stayed green. After the restore everything passed, and no NEUTERED marker remains.

Register: I removed src/context.rs from UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS, so it went from 2 to 1. The only one left is src/skill_fence.rs (#1040).

Docs: in permissions.md, I replaced the sentence saying /spawn ignores the /cd deny list (#1042) with the new behaviour. I also added an entry to ARCHITECTURE.md. The module-size gate gave only warnings that don't fail the run.

Still unfenced: /context's file list (list_project_context_files) still reads files without any fence. It never calls check_path, so the register guard can't see it. This is noted in the commit and in ARCHITECTURE.md.

I skipped the optional live pty canary run, so the fix is checked only by the unit tests, not by running the binary.
